From 4115194b7d86c28a5bd9990d7f4e2391a2262f98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eric Yang <eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:28:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perform_scan: Fix rescan retaining removed configs

A templated configuration (for example one named per GPU index) needs
its resolved name to survive an unrelated rescan, so that other configs
depending on it via FOUND keep matching. Commit "bugfix: perform_scan:
fix templated config issues" (e76d6d6) [1] achieved this by treating
every already-applied configuration's name as matched at the start of
each scan, regardless of what the current scan actually finds.

This also protects the name of a device that has genuinely been removed,
for example a blade that is physically removed (hot-unplug) or whose
chassis is powered off, either way losing its FRU EEPROM connection. Its
name is treated as matched purely from history, so the device is never
recognized as missing, its inventory is never removed, and anything that
only exists via FOUND of that inventory, such as a sensor or MCTP
endpoint, is left behind instead of being torn down.

Only treat a configuration's name as matched when this scan's probes
actually rediscover the device, instead of assuming every previously
applied name is still present. A device that is still there keeps its
FOUND dependents exactly as before; a device that is gone is now
correctly recognized as missing and removed along with anything
depending on it.

Tested:
Added unit tests for scan::detail::restorePersistedConfigurations.
- A rediscovered instance registers its saved resolved name and
  preserves its index.
- A removed device is left eligible for removal instead of retained by
  history (the regression this fixes).
- Removing one of several instances only affects that instance.
- A device known only from the cached configuration is registered the
  same as one already tracked in memory, once this scan rediscovers it.

Verified on a Yosemite 4 platform:
- Hot-removed Sentinel Dome Slot 7. Its chassis, CPU, and T1 board
  records (the 3 inventory objects tied to that slot) showed up in the
  missing list and were pruned;
  xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.InventoryRemoved events were emitted for
  all three inventory objects, and busctl tree confirmed they were gone
  while the other 7 slots were untouched.
- Hot-plugged Slot 7 back in. The same 3 records were rediscovered and
  re-registered, and xyz.openbmc_project.Inventory.InventoryAdded events
  were emitted for all three inventory objects, restoring the same
  topology as before removal.

Reference:
[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/87463

